title: Waiting a tick
mode: vhdl
chapter: Memory
check: behaves
cycles: 100
---- intro
Two flip-flops in a row delay a signal by two ticks: at each edge the second copies what the first **was** holding, just before the first changes.

In VHDL, both copies go in the same process. Every `<=` inside a clock edge uses the values from **before** the edge, so the order of the lines does not matter:

```
s1 <= d;
q  <= s1;
```

`s1` is a signal: the wire between the two flip-flops.
---- task
Make `q` show what `d` was two clock edges ago, using the signal `s1` as the first flip-flop.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity delay2 is
  port(
    clk : in  std_logic;
    d   : in  std_logic;
    q   : out std_logic
  );
end delay2;

architecture rtl of delay2 is
    signal s1 : std_logic := '0';
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity delay2 is
  port(
    clk : in  std_logic;
    d   : in  std_logic;
    q   : out std_logic
  );
end delay2;

architecture rtl of delay2 is
    signal s1 : std_logic := '0';
begin
    process (clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            s1 <= d;
            q  <= s1;
        end if;
    end process;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Both lines go inside the same `if rising_edge(clk) then`.
---- hint2
`s1 <= d;` and `q <= s1;`, inside the clock edge.
---- solution
```
process (clk)
begin
    if rising_edge(clk) then
        s1 <= d;
        q  <= s1;
    end if;
end process;
```
Set `d` to 1 and Step twice: `q` follows on the second step.
---- done
Swap the two lines and nothing changes: at the edge, all the flip-flops copy at once. Thinking of it as a program running line by line is the commonest VHDL mistake.
